  "evidence": "A: NHS funding to rise above inflation; recruit 92,000 nurses and 28,000 doctors; expand mental health support (school/college teams to 100%, early support hubs); 30 hours free childcare from 9 months; legal annual visa cap falling and Rwanda removals; mandatory National Service; tougher sentencing and new protest restrictions; defence spending to 2.5% of GDP by 2030. B: 2m extra NHS operations/scans/appointments per year and 40,000 extra weekly appointments; create a National Care Service and add 8,500 mental health staff; Warm Homes Plan to upgrade 5m homes; New Deal for Working People to improve job security/pay; neighbourhood policing and standards reforms without protest bans; reduce migration by linking visas to domestic training and a Border Security Command; defence/NATO commitments with a path to 2.5% of GDP.",
  "reasoning": "B prioritises Care/Harm through extensive NHS, social care, mental health, housing efficiency, and worker protections while still addressing Loyalty/Authority with policing and defence in a proportionate way, matching the electorate’s higher care and moderate loyalty/authority weights. A leans more heavily into Authority/Loyalty (national service, protest restrictions, migration caps and Rwanda, tougher sanctions) relative to its care measures, which is less aligned with the profile’s emphasis order.",
